@using System.Globalization
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Home";

    // The worked example below is regression case 1 — the broiler-starter formulation the database
    // seeds, exactly as the solver returns it at a 1000 kg batch. It is written here rather than
    // solved on request so the front page has no dependency on the optimizer or the database: a
    // failure in either should not be able to take down the page that explains what the app is.
    //
    // The trade is that these figures can drift away from the solver if the seed data ever changes,
    // so they live in this one block and nowhere else in the file, and every one of them is checked
    // against a live solve of the same formulation whenever this page is touched.
    //
    // Formatting mirrors _FormulationResults exactly, which is the point: this specimen has to be
    // the same artefact the app produces, not a picture of one. Percentages are formatted twice
    // from a single value — invariant for the CSS flex-basis, "F2" for the key the reader sees —
    // because a culture that writes 42,24 would hand CSS a length it silently discards.
    var totalCostText = "231.03";
    var costPerKgText = "0.2310";
    var batchText = "1000";
    var usedText = "3 / 4";

    double pctMaize = 42.2414;
    double pctSoy = 2.1552;
    double pctMustard = 55.6034;
}

<div class="container mt-4">

    @*
        The hero states the thesis rather than repeating the wordmark — that is already in the band
        forty pixels above. What an examiner needs first is what this program computes.

        Two columns on a wide screen, stacked below lg. The lede is deliberately short: the diagram
        beside it says "linear program" faster than a paragraph can, so the paragraph only has to
        say what that buys you.
    *@
    <div class="fc-hero row g-4 g-lg-5 align-items-center">
        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="fc-eyebrow">Least-cost feed formulation</div>
            <h1>The cheapest mix that meets every target.</h1>
            <p class="fc-lede">
                FeedCraft states a ration as a linear program and solves it exactly, so the mix it
                returns is the cheapest one that exists &mdash; not the best one found by trial. Then
                it reads the solution back: which requirements are holding the price up, and what one
                more unit of each is worth.
            </p>
            <div class="fc-actions">
                <a class="btn btn-primary" asp-area="" asp-controller="Feed" asp-action="Index">Start formulating</a>
                <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary" asp-area="" asp-controller="Knowledge" asp-action="Index">Browse the knowledge base</a>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="col-lg-6">
            @* Supplied artwork, so the page adds no words to it: the legend, the axis labels and the
               note are all inside the picture. Only the alt text is written here, and that is for a
               reader who cannot see it.

               width and height are the intrinsic size, so the browser reserves the box before the
               file arrives and the column below does not jump. srcset goes through Url.Content by
               hand — "~/" is expanded by UrlResolutionTagHelper, which only knows a fixed list of
               attributes, and srcset is not on it. sizes tracks the column: 46vw lands within a few
               pixels of col-lg-6 at every breakpoint above lg, and below lg the figure is
               full width. *@
            <figure class="fc-plate">
                <img src="~/img/feasible-region-1536.jpg"
                     srcset="@(Url.Content("~/img/feasible-region-768.jpg")) 768w, @(Url.Content("~/img/feasible-region-1536.jpg")) 1536w"
                     sizes="(min-width: 992px) 46vw, 100vw"
                     width="1536" height="1024"
                     alt="A graph of maize percentage against soybean meal percentage. Straight lines mark the nutrient requirements and enclose a shaded triangle holding every mix that satisfies all of them. Dashed lines join mixes that cost the same, an arrow shows cost falling towards the bottom left, and a diamond marks the corner of the triangle where the cheapest mix sits." />
            </figure>
        </div>
    </div>

    @*
        The specimen. Deliberately the app's own batch ticket, class for class, so the front page
        shows the real output vocabulary instead of a hero invented for the occasion. site.js already
        selects .fc-mixbar and [data-fc-countup] on every page, so the count-up and the staggered bar
        reveal arrive here with no new script — the same single moment of motion a real solve gets.
    *@
    <figure class="fc-specimen">
        <div class="fc-ticket">
            <div class="fc-ticket-head">
                <h2 class="fc-ticket-title">Worked example</h2>
                <span class="fc-stamp">Broiler starter</span>
            </div>

            <div class="fc-ticket-readout">
                <div class="fc-figures">
                    <div class="fc-figure fc-figure-hero">
                        <div class="fc-figure-value fc-num" data-fc-countup="@totalCostText">
                            <span class="fc-cur">৳</span><span data-fc-countup-out>@totalCostText</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="fc-figure-label">Total cost</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="fc-figure">
                        <div class="fc-figure-value fc-num">
                            <span class="fc-cur">৳</span>@costPerKgText
                        </div>
                        <div class="fc-figure-label">Per kg</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="fc-figure">
                        <div class="fc-figure-value fc-num">@batchText</div>
                        <div class="fc-figure-label">Batch, kg</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="fc-figure">
                        <div class="fc-figure-value fc-num">@usedText</div>
                        <div class="fc-figure-label">Ingredients used</div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                @* aria-hidden for the same reason it is on the real ticket: the key below states
                   every name and figure in it as text, so announcing four empty divs would only add
                   noise. The fourth segment is the hatched tick an ingredient the solver priced out
                   to zero keeps — "this was available and the LP rejected it" is a result. *@
                <div class="fc-mixbar" aria-hidden="true">
                    <div class="fc-seg" style="flex-basis: @pctMaize.ToString("0.####", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)%"></div>
                    <div class="fc-seg" style="flex-basis: @pctSoy.ToString("0.####", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)%"></div>
                    <div class="fc-seg" style="flex-basis: @pctMustard.ToString("0.####", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)%"></div>
                    <div class="fc-seg fc-seg-out"></div>
                </div>

                <ul class="fc-mixkey">
                    <li class="fc-key">
                        <span class="fc-swatch"></span>
                        Maize
                        <span class="fc-num">@pctMaize.ToString("F2")%</span>
                    </li>
                    <li class="fc-key">
                        <span class="fc-swatch"></span>
                        Soybean Meal
                        <span class="fc-num">@pctSoy.ToString("F2")%</span>
                    </li>
                    <li class="fc-key">
                        <span class="fc-swatch"></span>
                        Mustard Meal
                        <span class="fc-num">@pctMustard.ToString("F2")%</span>
                    </li>
                    <li class="fc-key fc-key-out">
                        <span class="fc-swatch"></span>
                        Vegetable Oil
                        <span class="fc-num">0.00%</span>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="fc-ticket-body">
                <h5>What's driving the cost</h5>
                @* The quiet half of the signature, and the part worth noticing: two of the five
                   nutrient targets are sitting on their bounds, and the dual solution prices them.
                   Same flag edge the pinned rows carry on the results page. *@
                <ul class="fc-notes mb-0">
                    <li class="fc-note fc-note-bind">
                        <strong>Crude Protein</strong> is pinned at its maximum,
                        <span class="fc-num">24.00</span>% &mdash; worth
                        <span class="fc-num">৳2.59</span> per tonne, per 1%.
                    </li>
                    <li class="fc-note fc-note-bind">
                        <strong>Lysine</strong> is pinned at its minimum,
                        <span class="fc-num">1.00</span>% &mdash; worth
                        <span class="fc-num">৳94.83</span> per tonne, per 1%.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <figcaption class="fc-specimen-note">
            The example formulation the app ships with, as the solver returns it. Open it to change
            any price or target and solve again.
        </figcaption>
    </figure>

    @*
        The hub. An eyebrow-set h2 so the heading order stays readable while the label keeps the
        smallest structural treatment — this is a signpost, not a second headline.
    *@
    <div class="fc-sections">
        <h2 class="fc-eyebrow">Sections</h2>

        <div class="row g-3">
            @* Formulate leads because it is what the app is for. It is marked with the mill-green
               edge rather than made larger: --fc-maize is spent on the brand mark and the
               composition bar, and a third place would stop it meaning anything. *@
            <div class="col-sm-6 col-lg-4">
                <a class="fc-tile fc-tile-lead h-100" asp-area="" asp-controller="Feed" asp-action="Index">
                    <h3 class="fc-tile-title">Formulate</h3>
                    <p class="fc-tile-note">
                        Set the batch size, the prices you can buy at and the nutrient targets you
                        have to hit, then solve.
                    </p>
                </a>
            </div>
            <div class="col-sm-6 col-lg-4">
                <a class="fc-tile h-100" asp-area="" asp-controller="Ingredients" asp-action="Index">
                    <h3 class="fc-tile-title">Ingredient Library</h3>
                    <p class="fc-tile-note">
                        The ingredients and nutrients a formulation starts from, with their prices
                        and composition.
                    </p>
                </a>
            </div>
            <div class="col-sm-6 col-lg-4">
                <a class="fc-tile h-100" asp-area="" asp-controller="Knowledge" asp-action="Index">
                    <h3 class="fc-tile-title">Knowledge Base</h3>
                    <p class="fc-tile-note">
                        Published feeding specifications by species and stage, ready to load onto a
                        formulation as its targets.
                    </p>
                </a>
            </div>
            <div class="col-sm-6 col-lg-4">
                <a class="fc-tile h-100" asp-area="" asp-controller="Experiment" asp-action="Index">
                    <h3 class="fc-tile-title">Experiment</h3>
                    <p class="fc-tile-note">
                        Solve two formulations side by side and see what the difference between them
                        costs.
                    </p>
                </a>
            </div>
            <div class="col-sm-6 col-lg-4">
                <a class="fc-tile h-100" asp-area="" asp-controller="Market" asp-action="Index">
                    <h3 class="fc-tile-title">Market</h3>
                    <p class="fc-tile-note">
                        What dealers are asking for each ingredient, searchable and sorted by price.
                    </p>
                </a>
            </div>
            @* Dealers only, on the same check the nav band uses. Presentation, not security —
               ListingsController carries [Authorize(Roles = ...)]. *@
            @if (User.IsInRole(FeedCraftDbContext.DealerRole))
            {
                <div class="col-sm-6 col-lg-4">
                    <a class="fc-tile h-100" asp-area="" asp-controller="Listings" asp-action="Index">
                        <h3 class="fc-tile-title">My Listings</h3>
                        <p class="fc-tile-note">
                            The ingredients you sell and the prices buyers see on the market page.
                        </p>
                    </a>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="fc-note">
        FeedCraft is a final-year Computer Science &amp; Engineering project.
        <a asp-area="" asp-controller="Home" asp-action="About">Read about the project and the two of us</a>.
    </p>

</div>
